window.STUDY_CHAPTERS=[{"id":1,"u":1,"from":1,"to":9,"title":"The OOP way of thinking","sub":"From procedures to objects","intro":"An object combines information and the operations that act on it. A class describes that structure; an object is one actual instance. C++ supports object-oriented as well as procedural programming.","sections":[["The four pillars","Encapsulation groups state and methods inside a class and controls access. Abstraction exposes what a user needs while hiding implementation details. Inheritance allows a derived class to reuse accessible behavior. Polymorphism lets one interface support different behavior."],["Why use OOP?","Modularity separates responsibilities into classes. Reusability reduces repetition; controlled access protects invariants; maintenance is easier when each object has a clear responsibility. OOP is used in GUI software, games, simulations and real-time applications."],["Class versus object","A Student class describes fields such as roll and marks and operations such as display(). Two Student objects can have different marks. A class definition establishes a type; ordinary non-static data is stored in instances."]],"table":[["Aspect","POP","OOP"],["Focus","Functions and procedures","Objects and responsibilities"],["Data","Often passed among functions","Grouped with related methods"],["Reuse","Functions","Composition, inheritance, functions"],["Examples","C (procedural style)","C++, Java (OOP styles)"]],"exam":["Define a class and an object. Explain four pillars of OOP with examples.","A class is a blueprint; each object is an instance. Explain each pillar in one or two sentences and give a Student, Vehicle or Shape example."],"mistake":"Don't say OOP always makes every program faster or that classes themselves can never use memory. Static data belongs to the class, not each instance.","lab":7},{"id":2,"u":1,"from":10,"to":17,"title":"Your first C++ program","sub":"Streams, syntax and program structure","intro":"C++ is a general-purpose language supporting object-oriented, procedural and generic programming. A conventional hosted C++ program starts execution in int main(). The standard library supplies the console streams.","sections":[["Program structure","Include necessary headers, declare names or use explicit std:: qualification, define functions or classes, and provide int main(). Braces delimit scopes and semicolons terminate most statements. Comments use // for a line or /* ... */ for a block."],["Input and output","<iostream> declares std::cin and std::cout. The extraction operator >> reads formatted values; insertion << writes values. Chaining operations allows several values to be read or printed in order. std::endl inserts a newline and flushes the stream; '\\n' usually suffices."],["Variables and main","Declare a variable before using it, choose a suitable type, and initialize it before reading its value. Returning 0 from main reports successful execution to the operating system, not a printed zero."]],"table":[["Part","Purpose"],["#include <iostream>","Access console-stream declarations"],["int main()","Entry point"],["cin >> x","Read a value"],["cout << x","Print a value"],["return 0;","Report success"]],"exam":["Explain the structure of a C++ program with an example.","Show a header, int main(), a declaration, input or output and the return statement. Explain the role of each."],"mistake":"Use int main(), not void main(), for standard hosted C++.","lab":0},{"id":3,"u":1,"from":18,"to":23,"title":"Tokens, types and references","sub":"The building blocks of C++","intro":"A token is a meaningful lexical unit of a program. Keywords are reserved words; identifiers are names chosen by programmers; literals denote fixed values; operators and punctuation carry syntactic meaning.","sections":[["Types and constants","Basic types include int, char, bool, float and double. Derived types include arrays, pointers and references. User-defined classes represent domain-specific types. A symbolic constant such as const int LIMIT = 10 names a fixed value."],["Declaration and initialization","int age; declares a local integer without initializing its value. int age = 18; initializes it. Dynamic initialization uses an expression evaluated at runtime, for example int total = quantity * price."],["Reference variables","A reference is an alias for an existing object. int &r = x; binds r to x. Assigning through r changes x. An ordinary reference must be initialized, and it cannot later be reseated to refer to another object."]],"table":[["Code","Category / meaning"],["int","Keyword"],["total","Identifier"],["25","Integer literal"],["const double PI = 3.14","Named constant"],["int *p","Pointer"],["int &r = x","Reference alias"]],"exam":["Differentiate a pointer and a reference.","A pointer is an object that can store an address and can be reassigned; a reference aliases an object and must be bound at initialization."],"mistake":"A reference is not simply an independently stored copy. Do not read an uninitialized automatic variable.","lab":1},{"id":4,"u":1,"from":24,"to":27,"title":"Operators and their effects","sub":"Arithmetic, relational and logical operations","intro":"Operators express calculations and tests. The operand types and operator determine the result; expressions can also modify state.","sections":[["Arithmetic and comparison","+, -, *, / and % perform arithmetic; % works with integral operands. Relational operators (<, >, <=, >=, ==, !=) compare values and produce bool results. Integer division truncates toward zero."],["Logical and assignment operators","&&, || and ! combine conditions. Built-in && and || short-circuit. = assigns a value; += and -= combine an operation and assignment. Use == for equality, not =."],["Increment, decrement and bitwise","++x increments then yields the updated value; x++ yields the old value and increments x. &, |, ^ and ~ operate on integer bit patterns; << and >> can be bit shifts for integer operands, distinct from stream usage."]],"table":[["Expression","If x starts at 5"],["x + 2","7; x remains 5"],["x += 2","x becomes 7"],["++x","Result 6, x becomes 6"],["x++","Result 5, x becomes 6"],["x == 5","true"]],"exam":["Explain pre-increment and post-increment with an example.","For x=5, ++x yields 6; x++ yields 5 before x becomes 6. Show separate examples."],"mistake":"Don't use assignment = in place of comparison ==. Avoid expressions that modify the same variable multiple times in an unclear evaluation order.","lab":null},{"id":5,"u":1,"from":28,"to":32,"title":"Special operators","sub":"Scope, member access, memory and conversions","intro":"C++ offers special operators for selecting names, accessing class members, allocating dynamic storage and converting types.","sections":[["Scope and member access",":: selects a name from a particular scope, such as Student::show or ::total for a global variable. Use obj.member for a direct object and ptr->member for a pointer to an object; ptr->member is shorthand for (*ptr).member."],["Dynamic memory","new constructs an object in dynamic storage and returns a pointer. delete destroys a single allocated object and releases storage. Pair new[] with delete[]. Modern C++ generally prefers containers or smart pointers for owning memory."],["Manipulators and type casts","Manipulators such as setw and setprecision affect stream formatting. A cast explicitly converts a value: static_cast<double>(sum) / count avoids unintended integer division. Conversions can lose information, such as fractional values when converting to int."]],"table":[["Operator","Example","Meaning"],["::","Student::show()","Qualified name"],[".","s.show()","Object member"],["->","p->show()","Pointer member"],["new / delete","new int(5) / delete p","Dynamic allocation"],["static_cast","static_cast<double>(n)","Explicit conversion"]],"exam":["Write a C++ example using scope resolution and explain new/delete.","Show an outside member definition or a global/local name example. Explain matching allocation and release forms."],"mistake":"Use delete[] for new[]. Avoid dereferencing a pointer after its allocation has been released.","lab":2},{"id":6,"u":1,"from":33,"to":35,"title":"Expressions and precedence","sub":"Read an expression correctly","intro":"An expression combines operands and operators to produce a value or an effect. Precedence groups operators; associativity resolves grouping at the same precedence level. Neither generally specifies the order in which operands are evaluated.","sections":[["Common expression types","Arithmetic expressions calculate values, relational expressions compare, logical expressions combine conditions, and assignment expressions modify a variable. Mixed-type expressions may convert operands to a common type."],["Grouping rules","Multiplication, division and remainder group before addition/subtraction; comparisons generally group before logical AND and OR; assignment has lower precedence. Parentheses make intention explicit."],["Worked trace","2 + 3 * 4 groups as 2 + (3 * 4) and evaluates to 14. (2 + 3) * 4 evaluates to 20. For a=5 and b=8, (a < b) && (b > 0) is true."]],"table":[["Higher to lower (selected groups)","Examples"],["Unary","!, ++, --"],["Multiplicative","*, /, %"],["Additive","+ , -"],["Relational / equality","<, >, ==, !="],["Logical","&& before ||"],["Assignment","=, +="]],"exam":["Explain operator precedence with two examples.","State that higher-precedence operators group first; show the result of 2+3*4 and (2+3)*4."],"mistake":"Do not equate precedence with all evaluation-order rules. Use parentheses for readability.","lab":null},{"id":7,"u":1,"from":36,"to":40,"title":"Control structures","sub":"Sequence, selection and iteration","intro":"Control structures determine which statements run and how many times. Sequence runs statements in order, selection chooses a branch, and iteration repeats a block.","sections":[["Selection","if executes a block when its condition is true; if-else chooses between alternatives; else-if handles multiple tests. switch selects a case using an integral or enumeration expression, and break usually prevents fall-through."],["Loops","for is convenient when the iteration count or update is known. while checks the condition before entering the body. do-while checks afterward, so its body runs at least once."],["Tracing a loop","For for(int i=1; i<=3; ++i), i starts at 1; the condition is checked; the body executes; ++i updates i. It prints 1, 2, 3 before the condition fails. Input may be chained as cin >> a >> b."]],"table":[["Structure","Condition checked","Minimum runs"],["if","Before branch","0 for that branch"],["while","Before body","0"],["for","Before body","0"],["do-while","After body","1"]],"exam":["Compare while and do-while. Write a loop printing 1 to 5.","Explain entry-controlled versus exit-controlled. Use for(int i=1;i<=5;++i) cout<<i;."],"mistake":"Ensure the loop changes the condition variables. A missing break in switch can run the next case.","lab":null},{"id":8,"u":2,"from":1,"to":4,"title":"Function basics and argument passing","sub":"Pass by value, reference and return by reference","intro":"A function packages a task behind a name, return type and parameters. A prototype declares its interface before use; a definition supplies the body.","sections":[["The main function and prototyping","int main() is the program entry point. A prototype int sum(int,int); lets the compiler check argument and return types. A call such as sum(3,4) supplies actual arguments, and the function returns its computed value."],["Call by value versus reference","A value parameter receives a separate value, so changing it does not change the caller's integer. A reference parameter such as int &x aliases the caller's object. Reference passing also avoids copying large objects."],["Return by reference","A reference return allows the caller to use the referred object as an alias, for example int& first(int &a){return a;}. The object must outlive that returned reference. A function must not return a reference to a destroyed local variable."]],"table":[["Mechanism","Parameter / return","Effect"],["By value","void f(int x)","Caller value unchanged by assignment to x"],["By reference","void f(int &x)","Assignments affect caller"],["Return by reference","int& f(int &x)","Caller can use returned alias"]],"exam":["Write a C++ program to swap two integers by reference.","Use int &a and int &b parameters, a temporary variable, and show that caller variables exchange values."],"mistake":"A C++ reference is an alias, not an explicit pointer parameter. Never return a reference to a local automatic variable.","lab":4},{"id":9,"u":2,"from":5,"to":8,"title":"Inline, default, const and overloaded functions","sub":"Make interfaces expressive","intro":"Functions can be declared inline, given default arguments, constrained with const, or overloaded to support different inputs. These facilities change how a function is called or defined, not its basic purpose.","sections":[["Inline functions","The inline specifier allows compatible definitions in multiple translation units and historically suggests expansion at a call site. A compiler may inline functions with or without inline; the keyword does not guarantee substitution. Small square functions are common teaching examples."],["Default and constant arguments","Default arguments supply missing trailing arguments, e.g. int add(int a,int b=5). A const reference parameter such as const Student& s avoids copying while restricting ordinary modification through that reference."],["Function overloading","Two functions may have the same name but distinguishable parameter lists. Overload resolution selects a suitable function using argument types. int add(int,int) and double add(double,double) can coexist; return type alone is insufficient."]],"table":[["Concept","Example","Remember"],["inline","inline int square(int x)","Not a guaranteed optimization"],["Default","int add(int a,int b=5)","Trailing omitted arguments"],["const","void show(const Box& b)","No ordinary mutation through b"],["Overload","show(int), show(double)","Different parameter lists"]],"exam":["Define function overloading and explain it with a suitable example.","Define same-name, distinguishable signatures; show int and double versions and which call chooses each."],"mistake":"Don't overload solely by return type. Default arguments can also make otherwise distinct overloads ambiguous.","lab":6},{"id":10,"u":2,"from":9,"to":13,"title":"Classes, objects and access","sub":"Your first reusable class","intro":"A class is a user-defined type containing data members and member functions. Access specifiers determine who can use members.","sections":[["Class specification","Declare the class with class Name { ... };. private restricts access to members and friends, public provides an interface, protected also permits appropriate derived-class access. A class defaults to private access."],["Object creation","Student s1; constructs an object. Use the dot operator for public members: s1.display(). Each object keeps its own non-static data. A function declared in the class can operate on the object's state."],["Defining members","A member function can be defined inside the class or declared there and defined outside with ClassName::function(). An inside definition is typically implicitly inline. An outside definition can explicitly use inline."]],"table":[["Access","Ordinary outside code","Derived class (appropriate object)"],["public","Yes","Yes"],["protected","No","Yes"],["private","No","No direct access"]],"exam":["Define class and object. Demonstrate a member function outside a class.","Show class Student { public: void show(); }; followed by void Student::show(){...}, then create and call an object in main."],"mistake":"Remember the semicolon after the class definition and the class qualification on outside member functions.","lab":7},{"id":11,"u":2,"from":14,"to":17,"title":"Member calls and object memory","sub":"Encapsulation in practice","intro":"Members cooperate to implement a class's public behavior. Private functions help enforce internal rules, and each object has its own non-static state.","sections":[["Nesting member functions","A member can call another member of the same class directly, for example display() calling calculate(). This allows reusable internal operations without exposing implementation to callers."],["Private member functions","A private function is not callable by arbitrary external code. A public method can validate input by calling a private helper. Friends have explicitly granted access."],["Object memory","Two Student objects hold independent non-static fields. A member-function implementation is shared code rather than duplicated for each instance. Static data belongs to the class. Object storage may also contain padding and implementation-specific data."]],"table":[["Member kind","Stored separately per object?"],["Non-static data","Yes"],["Static data","No; class-wide"],["Member function code","No separate code copy per object"]],"exam":["Explain nesting and private member functions with an example.","Show a public display() calling a private calculate() method; explain controlled access and code reuse."],"mistake":"Private does not mean inaccessible to the class itself. Do not assume object size is exactly the sum of visible fields.","lab":7},{"id":12,"u":2,"from":18,"to":19,"title":"Static members and arrays of objects","sub":"Shared versus individual state","intro":"A static data member is associated with a class rather than one particular object. An array of objects stores a sequence of instances of one type.","sections":[["Static data and static functions","Declare static int count; in the class and, for a non-inline member, provide a definition such as int Student::count=0; outside. A static member function can be called as Student::getCount() and has no this pointer."],["Class-wide counter","If two objects each increment a static count in their constructor, both observe the same count. A static member function needs an explicit object to access that object's non-static members."],["Array of objects","Student s[3]; creates three Student objects when a suitable default constructor exists. Use a loop to accept or display records: s[i].input() and s[i].display(). Arrays contain distinct objects indexed from zero."]],"table":[["Declaration","Meaning"],["static int count;","One shared member"],["static int getCount();","No implicit this"],["Student s[3];","Three separate objects"],["s[1].show();","Operate on second object"]],"exam":["Explain static data member and static member function with a program.","Define a shared count and a static getter. Construct two objects and demonstrate the shared result."],"mistake":"A static member function cannot access a non-static member without an object. Standard C++ does not support runtime-sized built-in arrays; use vector for dynamic counts.","lab":8},{"id":13,"u":2,"from":20,"to":22,"title":"Objects as arguments and friend functions","sub":"Choose access deliberately","intro":"Objects can be passed to functions by value, by reference or by pointer. A friend declaration explicitly authorizes a function or class to access otherwise restricted members.","sections":[["Passing objects","By value creates a separate object (subject to copying/moving rules); by reference operates on an existing object; by const reference avoids copying and restricts mutation; a pointer can be null and uses -> to access members when valid."],["Friend functions","Declare friend void show(Box b); inside Box. A non-member friend can read private members without being a member itself, and is called like an ordinary function. Friendship is granted explicitly."],["When to use friendship","It can be useful for symmetric binary operators or a closely related helper. Prefer a public interface when appropriate to preserve encapsulation; friendship is not inherited or automatically reciprocal."]],"table":[["Pass style","Example"],["Value","void f(Box b)"],["Reference","void f(Box& b)"],["Const reference","void f(const Box& b)"],["Pointer","void f(Box* p)"]],"exam":["Explain friend function with a suitable example.","Declare a friend in a class with a private data member, define the non-member friend and call it on an object."],"mistake":"A non-member friend has no implicit this pointer. The function is not called as b.show() merely because it is a friend.","lab":10},{"id":14,"u":2,"from":23,"to":26,"title":"Returning objects and const methods","sub":"Safe interfaces and object lifetime","intro":"Functions can return class objects by value, or return references to objects with appropriate lifetimes. A const-qualified member function promises not to ordinarily modify the object through this.","sections":[["Return an object by value","Box makeBox(){return Box(5);} creates a result object. Modern C++ often constructs the result directly through copy elision. Returning by value is generally safe for a newly created local result."],["Return an existing object","A reference return is useful for an existing object with a sufficiently long lifetime. A pointer return requires a clear ownership and lifetime policy; dynamic allocation should not be used without a cleanup plan."],["Const member functions","int get() const { return value; } is callable on const objects. Inside such a function, this points to a const-qualified object. Mutable members are a special exception, not a general reason to modify state."]],"table":[["Return / declaration","Use"],["Box make()","New result value"],["Box& get()","Alias to living object"],["int get() const","Read-only member interface"]],"exam":["Explain returning objects and const member functions.","Show a factory returning a value and a getter declared const. State the lifetime restriction on reference returns."],"mistake":"Never return a reference or pointer to an ordinary local object that is about to be destroyed.","lab":null},{"id":15,"u":3,"from":1,"to":5,"title":"Constructors and overloading","sub":"How objects come to life","intro":"A constructor initializes a class object as its lifetime begins. It has the class name and no return type. Multiple constructors can support different initialization patterns.","sections":[["Default and parameterized constructors","A default constructor can be called with no arguments. A parameterized constructor receives values for initialization. Member initializer lists, e.g. Box(int w):width(w){}, initialize members before the constructor body."],["Constructor overloading","Box() and Box(int) support different calls. Constructors with default arguments can also be called without those arguments. Design the signatures so calls are not ambiguous."],["Initialization order","Non-static data members initialize in declaration order, regardless of initializer-list order. Use initializer lists for const members and references. Constructors are called when objects are constructed, not every time a member function is called."]],"table":[["Constructor","Call"],["Box()","Box b;"],["Box(int w)","Box b(6);"],["Box(int w=1)","Box b; or Box b(6);"]],"exam":["List and explain the types of constructors.","Define default, parameterized and copy constructors; include syntax and an example of each."],"mistake":"Constructors have no return type, not even void. Box b(); may declare a function rather than an object.","lab":11},{"id":16,"u":3,"from":6,"to":8,"title":"Dynamic initialization and copying","sub":"Object values, ownership and copy constructors","intro":"Objects may be initialized using values known only at runtime. A copy constructor initializes a new object from an existing object of the same class.","sections":[["Dynamic initialization","Read a value from the user and pass it to a constructor, such as Box b(width). The object is initialized with a runtime value, not necessarily allocated dynamically."],["Copy constructor","A common signature is Box(const Box& other). Copy each required member. For an ordinary integer member, b.x = other.x copies its value. Copy initialization creates a new object; assignment changes an already existing object."],["Dynamic constructor (syllabus terminology)","The notes use dynamic constructor for a constructor that allocates memory with new. If the object owns raw memory, a shallow pointer copy can lead to shared ownership mistakes or double deletion. Prefer RAII containers or implement correct copy and destruction semantics."]],"table":[["Concept","Meaning"],["Dynamic initialization","Runtime initialization values"],["Copy construction","New object from another object"],["Dynamic storage","Memory allocated with new"],["Assignment","Modify an existing object"]],"exam":["Explain copy constructor and dynamic constructor with examples.","Give Box(const Box& b) and explain independent copied state. For dynamic allocation, show matching cleanup."],"mistake":"Dynamic initialization is not the same as heap allocation. Copying a pointer is not a deep copy of its pointed-to data.","lab":12},{"id":17,"u":3,"from":9,"to":9,"title":"Destructors and lifetime","sub":"Cleanup when an object ends","intro":"A destructor performs cleanup at the end of an object's lifetime. Its name is the class name prefixed with ~; it has no return type or ordinary parameters.","sections":[["Automatic cleanup","Objects with automatic storage are destroyed on leaving their scope, in reverse order of completed construction. Destructors run even when returning normally from a function or unwinding an exception."],["Dynamic objects","delete p destroys the object and releases the matching allocation; delete[] destroys an allocated array. A destructor should release resources the object owns. RAII associates resource lifetime with object lifetime."],["Constructor versus destructor","A constructor establishes the object state. A destructor cleans up resources. Constructors can be overloaded; a class has a destructor rather than parameter-overloaded destructors."]],"table":[["Constructor","Destructor"],["ClassName(...)","~ClassName()"],["Initializes","Cleans up"],["Can take parameters","No ordinary parameters"],["Called on construction","Called at end of lifetime"]],"exam":["Differentiate constructor and destructor.","Compare names, purpose, parameters, overloading and timing. Trace nested scope construction/destruction."],"mistake":"Do not say a destructor directly frees every object's storage. Storage release depends on the object's storage duration and operation.","lab":13},{"id":18,"u":3,"from":10,"to":11,"title":"Why overload operators?","sub":"Readable operations on user-defined types","intro":"Operator overloading defines how existing operators behave when used with class or enumeration operands. It allows operations such as adding two Complex values to look natural.","sections":[["Definition and syntax","A member operator uses return_type operator+(parameters). For example, Number operator+(const Number& rhs) const can produce a new Number representing a sum. A non-member operator can take both operands explicitly."],["When it is appropriate","Overload an operator when its conventional meaning fits the type. Addition for distances or complex numbers is intuitive. Do not use an unexpected operation merely because overloading permits it."],["How the call works","For a + b with a suitable member overload, the call corresponds to a.operator+(b). Overload resolution selects a candidate. The return type depends on the operator's semantics and your implementation."]],"table":[["Expression","Member-style interpretation"],["a + b","a.operator+(b)"],["++a","a.operator++()"],["a == b","a.operator==(b)"]],"exam":["Define operator overloading. Write syntax for an overloaded + operator.","Explain reuse of an existing operator with a user-defined class and show Number operator+(const Number& rhs) const."],"mistake":"You cannot create a new operator symbol or change an operator's arity, precedence or associativity.","lab":15},{"id":19,"u":3,"from":12,"to":15,"title":"Unary and binary operators","sub":"Trace overloaded expressions","intro":"Unary operators act on one operand, and binary operators act on two. Overloaded member operators receive the current object implicitly; explicit parameters supply other operands.","sections":[["Unary example","A prefix increment member C& operator++(){++n;return *this;} modifies the object and returns it. The postfix form uses a dummy int parameter, e.g. C operator++(int), and normally returns the old value."],["Binary example","A member Number operator+(const Number& rhs) const uses the left object's value and rhs's value to construct a new result. For 4 and 5, the result is 9 while the original operands need not change."],["Rules from the notes","At least one operand must have a class or enumeration type for an overloaded operator. You cannot change precedence, associativity or number of operands; only overload permitted existing operators. Operators such as ::, ., .*, ?: and sizeof cannot be overloaded."]],"table":[["Kind","Member parameters","Example"],["Prefix unary","0","operator++()"],["Postfix ++","1 dummy int","operator++(int)"],["Binary","1","operator+(const N&)"],["Non-member binary","2","operator+(const N&,const N&)"]],"exam":["Differentiate unary and binary operator overloading; list four rules.","Explain operand counts and member parameter counts, give one code example of each, and list restrictions."],"mistake":"Prefix and postfix ++ use different signatures. Avoid changing the meaning of familiar arithmetic operations unexpectedly.","lab":14},{"id":20,"u":4,"from":1,"to":4,"title":"Base and derived classes","sub":"Inheritance and visibility","intro":"Inheritance allows a derived class to extend a base class. It is often used to model an is-a relationship and reuse accessible operations.","sections":[["Defining a derived class","class Car : public Vehicle { ... }; defines Car with Vehicle as a public base. A derived class contains a base subobject and can add its own state or behavior."],["Visibility modes","Public inheritance retains inherited public/protected access as public/protected. Protected inheritance makes inherited public/protected members protected. Private inheritance makes them private within the derived class. Private members of the base remain inaccessible directly to derived members."],["Controlled access","A base can expose a public getter/setter or protected member to permit controlled derived access. Do not simply make all fields public: maintain invariants through suitable member functions."]],"table":[["Base member","Public inheritance","Protected inheritance","Private inheritance"],["public","public","protected","private"],["protected","protected","protected","private"],["private","No direct access","No direct access","No direct access"]],"exam":["Explain visibility modes in inheritance with a table.","Define public, protected and private inheritance and show how the base's accessible members change."],"mistake":"Private members are still part of a base subobject; they are not directly accessible by a derived member.","lab":16},{"id":21,"u":4,"from":5,"to":12,"title":"Five inheritance patterns","sub":"Single, multiple, multilevel, hierarchical and hybrid","intro":"The number and arrangement of base classes determine the inheritance pattern. The diagrams describe type relationships, not the order in which statements execute.","sections":[["Single and multilevel","Single: Car has one direct base Vehicle. Multilevel: Dog derives from Mammal, which derives from Animal. Reuse can occur through several levels."],["Multiple and hierarchical","Multiple: AllInOne derives from Printer and Scanner. Hierarchical: Car and Bike derive separately from Vehicle. Multiple inheritance may require qualification when names from different bases conflict."],["Hybrid inheritance","A hybrid combines patterns. In the diamond A → B and C → D (where D derives from B and C), ordinary inheritance can create two A subobjects. Virtual inheritance can provide one shared virtual A base."]],"table":[["Type","Relationship"],["Single","A → B"],["Multilevel","A → B → C"],["Multiple","A + B → C"],["Hierarchical","A → B and C"],["Hybrid","Combination / diamond"]],"exam":["Explain any four types of inheritance with diagrams.","For each, draw named base/derived boxes and connecting arrows, then give one practical class example."],"mistake":"Multiple inheritance (many parents) is different from hierarchical inheritance (many children).","lab":17},{"id":22,"u":4,"from":13,"to":15,"title":"Virtual base classes and abstract classes","sub":"Resolve ambiguity and define interfaces","intro":"Virtual inheritance can share a common base subobject in a diamond. An abstract class defines an interface that cannot itself be directly instantiated.","sections":[["Diamond problem","If B and C both inherit A non-virtually and D inherits B and C, D generally contains two A subobjects. A member such as d.x may be ambiguous. B and C can virtually inherit A to provide one shared virtual A base."],["Abstract class","A class is abstract if it has a pure virtual function for which the final overrider remains pure. Syntax: virtual void draw() = 0;. A derived class must implement the required interface to become concrete."],["Why this matters","A Shape base can require draw() while Circle and Rectangle implement it differently. Virtual bases address shared inheritance structure; pure virtual functions address interface requirements. These are distinct mechanisms."]],"table":[["Concept","Solves"],["Virtual base","Duplicate shared base subobjects"],["Abstract class","Common interface / required behavior"],["Pure virtual member","Declares an abstract operation"]],"exam":["Explain virtual base class and abstract class with examples.","Draw a diamond and explain virtual A. Then show class Shape with virtual void draw()=0 and a concrete derived implementation."],"mistake":"Virtual inheritance is not the same as a virtual function. An abstract class can still have constructors and data members.","lab":20},{"id":23,"u":4,"from":16,"to":17,"title":"Constructors in derived classes","sub":"Base before derived","intro":"Creating a derived object initializes its base subobjects before its own members and constructor body. Destruction reverses the construction order.","sections":[["Default base constructor","If no base initializer is specified, a suitable default constructor is normally used for that base. If the base requires arguments, the derived constructor must supply them explicitly."],["Parameterized base constructor","Derived(int n): Base(n), value(n) {} initializes the base with n before the derived body executes. Member initialization follows declaration order."],["Multiple inheritance order","Non-virtual direct bases are initialized in the order listed in the class's base specification, not the order of names in the initializer list. Virtual bases are initialized first by the most-derived constructor."]],"table":[["Stage","Typical order"],["Construction","Virtual bases → direct bases → members → body"],["Destruction","Derived body → members → bases"]],"exam":["Explain order of constructor execution in inheritance.","Show A's constructor printing A and B's constructor printing B. B b; prints AB; explain base initialization first."],"mistake":"Changing the order in an initializer list does not change the actual base/member initialization order.","lab":21},{"id":24,"u":4,"from":18,"to":22,"title":"Exception handling","sub":"try, throw and catch","intro":"Exceptions provide a way to report abnormal conditions separately from normal processing. C++ transfers control from a throw expression to a matching handler.","sections":[["Basic mechanism","Place operations that may throw inside try. Use throw value; to signal an exception, and catch(Type name) to handle a matching type. If a throw occurs, the following normal statements in that try block are not executed."],["Control flow","The runtime searches enclosing handlers for a match. During stack unwinding, completed automatic objects in abandoned scopes are destroyed. After a handled exception, execution continues after the try/catch sequence."],["Division example","Before dividing, check if the divisor is zero; throw an appropriate exception, such as std::runtime_error, or an int in a simple teaching example. Catch it and show an error message. C++ does not automatically turn every programming error into a catchable exception."]],"table":[["Keyword","Purpose"],["try","Protect a region of code"],["throw","Signal an exceptional condition"],["catch","Handle a matching exception"]],"exam":["Explain exception handling mechanism with a program.","Show a try block, checked invalid input, throw and matching catch. Explain control transfer and continuation."],"mistake":"Don't assume invalid pointer access or integer division by zero is automatically caught by catch(...).","lab":22},{"id":25,"u":4,"from":23,"to":27,"title":"Multiple handlers and rethrowing","sub":"Match the error, then decide what to do","intro":"One try block may have multiple catch handlers. Matching is attempted in declaration order. A catch-all handler matches an exception not handled by preceding typed handlers.","sections":[["Multiple catch statements","Use handlers for relevant types, e.g. catch(int), catch(double) and catch(const std::exception&). Derived-class exceptions may match a suitable base-class handler; order more specific handlers first."],["Catch-all","catch(...) is useful as a last fallback when the exact type is unknown. It must appear after all typed handlers. Avoid silently swallowing errors when the program cannot recover."],["Rethrowing","Inside an active handler, throw; rethrows the currently handled exception. An outer try/catch may handle it. This is useful when an inner handler logs or performs local cleanup but cannot fully recover."]],"table":[["Thrown value","Possible handler"],["7","catch(int)"],["2.5","catch(double)"],["Unexpected exception","catch(...)"],["Already handling an exception","throw; rethrows it"]],"exam":["Explain multiple catch, catch-all and rethrowing.","Give an example with two typed handlers, catch(...) last, and demonstrate throw; within a handler."],"mistake":"Do not place catch(...) before typed catches. throw; outside an active exception handler is invalid at runtime.","lab":23},{"id":26,"u":5,"from":1,"to":2,"title":"Pointers and memory","sub":"Addresses, dereferencing and manipulation","intro":"A pointer stores a pointer value representing an address (or null). The address-of operator & obtains the address of an object; * dereferences a valid pointer.","sections":[["Declaring and initializing","int x=5; int *p=&x; declares p pointing to x. *p is the referred integer; assigning *p=9 changes x. nullptr represents a null pointer and must not be dereferenced."],["Pointer manipulation","For an array int a[3], p=&a[0] and ++p can advance to the next element. Pointer arithmetic is defined within the same array or one-past-the-end; one-past cannot be dereferenced."],["Address versus value","p stores a pointer value. &p is the address of the pointer object itself. *p reads or modifies the pointed-to object. Types determine what element and arithmetic are meaningful."]],"table":[["Expression","Meaning"],["&x","Address of x"],["p","Stored pointer value"],["*p","Pointed-to value"],["&p","Address of p"],["p + 1","Next element within array bounds"]],"exam":["Explain declaration, initialization and manipulation of pointers.","Trace int x=5; int* p=&x; *p=9; and show x becomes 9."],"mistake":"Never dereference null, dangling or one-past pointers. Pointer arithmetic is not arbitrary memory traversal.","lab":24},{"id":27,"u":5,"from":3,"to":5,"title":"Object pointers and this","sub":"Which object am I operating on?","intro":"A pointer to a class object can invoke accessible members. In a non-static member function, this identifies the object on which the function was called.","sections":[["Pointer to object","Student s; Student* p=&s; p->show(); invokes show on s. (*p).show() has the same meaning when p is valid. An array of objects can also be traversed using pointers within its bounds."],["The this pointer","In Box::set(int value), this->value=value distinguishes the data member from the parameter. this is available in non-static member functions, not in static member functions."],["Return the current object","Returning *this by reference can support chaining, as in Box& set(int n){value=n;return *this;}. The caller must not use such a reference after the object dies."]],"table":[["Syntax","Use"],["s.show()","Direct object"],["p->show()","Pointer to object"],["this->x","Current object's member"],["*this","Current object as an lvalue"]],"exam":["Explain this pointer with a program.","Show a constructor or setter with a parameter named x and use this->x=x to initialize the member."],"mistake":"this is not the class itself. It refers to the current object and is unavailable in static member functions.","lab":26},{"id":28,"u":5,"from":6,"to":9,"title":"Compile-time polymorphism","sub":"One name, several valid forms","intro":"Polymorphism means many forms. In the syllabus, compile-time polymorphism is demonstrated using function overloading and operator overloading.","sections":[["Function overloading","The compiler chooses an appropriate overload from argument types, e.g. print(int) versus print(double). The same name expresses a common operation on different inputs."],["Operator overloading","A user-defined type can define an operator such as + to combine objects. The compiler resolves which overload to call; this is not virtual dispatch by itself."],["Early binding","The selection depends on statically available types and overload rules. The term compile-time polymorphism contrasts with runtime selection of virtual overrides. The actual arithmetic or function body still executes at runtime."]],"table":[["Mechanism","Selection basis"],["Function overloading","Argument types and overload rules"],["Operator overloading","Operand types and overload rules"],["Virtual function","Dynamic type through appropriate pointer/reference"]],"exam":["Differentiate compile-time and run-time polymorphism.","Explain early overload selection versus dynamic dispatch and give one example of each."],"mistake":"Overloading and overriding are not interchangeable terms. Overloading alone does not choose a virtual override at runtime.","lab":6},{"id":29,"u":5,"from":10,"to":12,"title":"Run-time polymorphism and base pointers","sub":"Choose behavior using the actual object","intro":"A base-class pointer or reference can refer to an accessible derived object. Virtual member functions permit a call through the base interface to select the appropriate override at runtime.","sections":[["Why runtime dispatch?","A collection of Shape* values may refer to different concrete shapes. A single draw() call can execute the correct implementation without an explicit switch on every shape type."],["Static and dynamic type","For Base* p=&derived, the static pointer type is Base*. The dynamic object type is Derived. A nonvirtual Base member call is resolved through the base interface; a virtual call can dispatch to Derived's override."],["Pointer limitations","A Base* does not directly expose derived-only members. Use the shared base interface. Copying a Derived object by value into a Base object can slice away derived state; pointers and references preserve the referenced object's dynamic type."]],"table":[["Call through Base*","Actual object Derived"],["Nonvirtual show()","Base::show()"],["Virtual show(), overridden","Derived::show()"]],"exam":["Explain need for runtime polymorphism with a C++ program.","Use a base pointer pointing to a derived object, declare the base method virtual, override it and show the derived result."],"mistake":"Do not confuse function name hiding with a valid virtual override. Use override to catch signature mismatches.","lab":27},{"id":30,"u":5,"from":13,"to":14,"title":"Virtual and pure virtual functions","sub":"Interfaces that dispatch","intro":"A virtual function is a member function participating in dynamic dispatch. A pure virtual function is declared with =0; a class with a remaining pure final overrider is abstract.","sections":[["Virtual functions","Declare virtual void show(); in the base. In the derived class define void show() override { ... }. Matching cv qualifiers and parameter types are important for overriding."],["Pure virtual functions","virtual double area() const = 0; specifies a required interface. A concrete Rectangle must implement it. Shape cannot be instantiated directly while it remains abstract, but Shape* can point to a concrete derived object."],["Exam distinction","A virtual function may provide a default implementation. A pure virtual declaration makes the interface abstract until a concrete final overrider is supplied. An abstract base may have ordinary methods, state and constructors."]],"table":[["Virtual function","Pure virtual function"],["virtual void f();","virtual void f() = 0;"],["May have base implementation","Declares pure interface"],["Class can be concrete","Class abstract if still pure"]],"exam":["Differentiate virtual and pure virtual functions with syntax.","State the role of dynamic dispatch, show both declarations and an overriding derived method."],"mistake":"An abstract class is not a class containing only pure virtual functions; it may contain other members.","lab":28},{"id":31,"u":5,"from":15,"to":17,"title":"Virtual constructors and destructors","sub":"Factory patterns and safe cleanup","intro":"The teacher notes discuss virtual constructors as a design idea, but C++ constructors cannot themselves be virtual. Virtual destructors enable appropriate destruction through a polymorphic base interface.","sections":[["Virtual constructor idea","Construction must establish the actual object's type. C++ therefore does not allow a constructor declared virtual. A factory function can choose a concrete type, and a virtual clone() method can construct a copy of the dynamic type."],["Virtual destructor","If a derived object is deleted through an ordinary base pointer, a suitable virtual base destructor is required for correct polymorphic deletion. Example: virtual ~Base() = default;."],["Lifetime trace","Base* p = new Derived; delete p; with a virtual Base destructor first invokes Derived's destructor, then Base's destructor. Each class cleans up its owned resources."]],"table":[["Constructor","Destructor"],["Cannot be virtual in C++","Can be virtual"],["Factory/clone can emulate creation choice","Supports polymorphic destruction"],["Base then derived construction","Derived then base destruction"]],"exam":["Can a constructor be virtual? Explain virtual destructor.","Answer no for constructors in standard C++; discuss factory/clone and show virtual ~Base() with deletion through Base*."],"mistake":"Never claim that C++ supports virtual constructors directly.","lab":29},{"id":32,"u":6,"from":1,"to":4,"title":"Console streams and unformatted input","sub":"Read and write characters","intro":"C++ stream objects provide interfaces to input and output. Standard streams include cin, cout, cerr and clog; stream classes provide formatted and unformatted operations.","sections":[["Stream classes","istream handles input, ostream handles output, and iostream combines both. File-stream classes extend these capabilities to files. cin is standard input; cout is standard output; cerr/clog report diagnostics."],["Formatted versus unformatted","Extraction >> parses a typed value and usually skips leading whitespace; insertion << formats a value. Unformatted operations work with characters or character sequences rather than parsing a formatted number."],["Character and line functions","cin.get(c) reads a character including whitespace. cout.put(c) writes one character. std::getline(cin,s) reads a line into a string; ostream::write(buffer,n) writes n characters. Member getline can read into a character array with a size limit."]],"table":[["Operation","Purpose"],["cin >> n","Formatted numeric input"],["cin.get(c)","Read one character"],["cout.put(c)","Write one character"],["getline(cin,s)","Read a line"],["cout.write(buf,n)","Write n characters"]],"exam":["Explain get(), put(), getline() and write().","Give a definition and short syntax example of each. Contrast single-character and multi-character operations."],"mistake":"After formatted input, a pending newline can cause getline to read an empty line; consume it deliberately.","lab":null},{"id":33,"u":6,"from":5,"to":9,"title":"Formatted I/O and manipulators","sub":"Present output clearly","intro":"Formatted console I/O controls the appearance and interpretation of values. Manipulators from <iomanip> help set width, precision and alignment.","sections":[["Formatting output","setw(n) sets a minimum width for the next formatted insertion; it does not permanently set every width. setfill(ch) changes padding. left and right set alignment."],["Floating-point precision","setprecision(n) controls precision. With fixed, it specifies digits after the decimal point. Without fixed, it generally specifies significant digits. fixed << setprecision(2) is useful for prices."],["Worked result","cout << fixed << setprecision(2) << 3.14159; prints 3.14. cout << setw(5) << 42; normally produces three padding characters followed by 42. These affect displayed text, not the original numeric value."]],"table":[["Manipulator","Effect"],["setw(n)","Minimum field width for next insertion"],["setprecision(n)","Precision"],["fixed","Fixed-point format"],["setfill(c)","Padding character"],["left / right","Alignment"]],"exam":["Explain formatted I/O with examples of manipulators.","Write a short program using iomanip, setw and fixed/setprecision; state the output."],"mistake":"setprecision(2) without fixed does not necessarily mean two decimal places.","lab":30},{"id":34,"u":6,"from":10,"to":14,"title":"Files and file streams","sub":"Persistent information beyond a program run","intro":"A file stores data persistently. The <fstream> header supplies streams to read from and write to files.","sections":[["Three file-stream classes","ifstream is intended for input, ofstream for output, and fstream for input and output. Their stream state can be checked before processing."],["Opening a file","Use the constructor (ifstream in(\"notes.txt\");) or open() after declaring a stream. The file name and open mode determine behavior. Always verify the open succeeded with if(!in) or is_open()."],["Closing and RAII","close() disconnects the file from the stream. An automatic file stream closes on destruction, but explicit close is useful when a file must be reopened or an output operation completed before reading. Check stream state for failed writes."]],"table":[["Class","Typical use"],["ifstream","Read existing file"],["ofstream","Create/write file"],["fstream","Read and write"],["open()","Associate with file"],["close()","Close association"]],"exam":["Write a C++ program to write to and read from a file.","Use ofstream, check open, write text, close, open ifstream, read and display text."],"mistake":"Check both open and I/O results. Don't read from an ofstream.","lab":31},{"id":35,"u":6,"from":15,"to":17,"title":"File modes and end-of-file","sub":"Choose what opening a file does","intro":"File open modes control the purpose and behavior of a stream. End-of-file is a stream condition detected when an attempted input reaches the end.","sections":[["Open modes","ios::in opens for input; ios::out for output; ios::trunc discards existing contents; ios::app directs each write to the end; ios::ate seeks to the end initially; ios::binary opens in binary mode."],["Combine modes","Use | to combine compatible flags, e.g. ios::in | ios::out. ofstream normally opens for output and commonly truncates unless an appropriate mode is supplied. app preserves prior data and appends new output."],["EOF and successful reads","eof() reports the EOF state after an operation detects it. A loop should usually test the read itself: while(getline(in,line)) { ... }. This avoids processing stale or failed input."]],"table":[["Mode","Purpose"],["in","Input"],["out","Output"],["app","Write at end"],["ate","Initially seek to end"],["trunc","Discard prior contents"],["binary","Binary mode"]],"exam":["Differentiate ios::app and ios::ate. Explain EOF detection.","app makes writes append; ate only initially positions at end. Use a read-success loop."],"mistake":"Never use while(!in.eof()) as the usual file-reading pattern. app and ate are not synonyms.","lab":32},{"id":36,"u":6,"from":18,"to":21,"title":"File pointers and positioning","sub":"Seek, tell and default behavior","intro":"File streams maintain positions for reading (get) and writing (put). Positioning functions allow access to a particular location where supported.","sections":[["Get and put positions","tellg() reports the input position; tellp() reports the output position. seekg() moves the get position; seekp() moves the put position. Positions can be absolute or relative using ios::beg, ios::cur and ios::end where appropriate."],["Default actions","ifstream commonly opens for reading from the beginning. ofstream typically begins writing with truncation unless another mode changes that behavior. fstream can read and write depending on its modes."],["Worked trace","For a binary file containing ABCDE, seekg(2) positions input at the third byte; get(c) reads C. tellg() then normally reports the next position, 3. Text-mode offsets are more constrained and should be handled using valid stream positions."]],"table":[["Function","Position"],["tellg()","Report read position"],["tellp()","Report write position"],["seekg(pos)","Set read position"],["seekp(pos)","Set write position"]],"exam":["Explain file-pointer functions with an example.","Give tellg/tellp/seekg/seekp and trace reading the third character of a binary file."],"mistake":"Do not assume a seek offset is a line number. Clear relevant failure state before repositioning after a failed read.","lab":33},{"id":37,"u":6,"from":22,"to":25,"title":"Sequential file I/O and error handling","sub":"Make file programs reliable","intro":"Sequential I/O processes data from the current position. C++ file streams support character, text and block operations and expose states indicating success or failure.","sections":[["Character and block I/O","get(c) reads one character; put(c) writes one. read(buffer,n) attempts to read n characters into a sufficiently large buffer; write(buffer,n) writes n characters. In binary operations, avoid treating arbitrary class objects containing pointers as portable byte records."],["Stream state functions","good() checks whether no state flags are set; eof() reports end-of-file; fail() reports failed extraction/format or bad state; bad() reports a serious stream error. Testing if(stream) is convenient for ordinary I/O success."],["Safe program structure","Open and verify, perform operations while checking their results, handle errors, and close or let RAII close. For text, while(getline(in,line)) is usually correct. For a fixed-size block, check read success and gcount() if partial reads matter."]],"table":[["Function","Purpose"],["get / put","Single-character I/O"],["read / write","Character blocks"],["eof()","EOF flag"],["fail()","Failure state"],["bad()","Serious stream error"],["good()","No error flags"]],"exam":["Explain sequential I/O operations and error handling in C++.","Describe get, put, read, write and the stream-state functions; give a safe open/read loop."],"mistake":"Binary byte I/O is not a safe general-purpose serialization method for all C++ objects.","lab":34}];
